      "name": null,
      "problem": "If $b-5c=c-4b,$ then $b=$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to isolate the variable $b.$ To do this, we treat $c$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we add $4b$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b-5c & =c-4b \\\\ b-5c+4b & =c-4b+4b \\\\ b+4b-5c & =c-4b+4b \\\\ 5b-5c & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $5c$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5b-5c & =c \\\\ 5b-5c+5c & =c+5c \\\\ 5b & =6c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5b & =6c \\\\ \\frac{5b}{5} & =\\frac{6c}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}b}{\\cancel{5}} & =\\frac{6c}{5} \\\\ b & =\\frac{6c}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
