      "problem": "If $\\frac{z}{3}-2y=1$ then $z=$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to isolate the variable $z.$ To do this, we treat $y$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we add $2y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z}{3}-2y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{z}{3}-2y+2y & =1+2y \\\\ \\frac{z}{3} & =1+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z}{3} & =1+2y \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{z}{3} & =3\\cdot (1+2y) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot \\frac{z}{\\cancel{3}} & =3\\cdot (1+2y) \\\\ z & =3\\cdot (1+2y) \\\\ z & =3\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2y \\\\ z & =3+6y\\end{aligned}\n$$",
