      "dom_id": "question-176587",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that $2x-y=y-x,$ express $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We need to isolate the variable $x.$ To do this, we treat $y$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we add $y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =y-x \\\\ 2x-y+y & =y-x+y \\\\ 2x & =-x+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-x+2y \\\\ 2x+x & =-x+2y+x \\\\ 3x & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =2y \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{2y}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}x}{\\cancel{3}} & =\\frac{2y}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2y}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
